Some conversions fail. Text may not contain a valid number, an integer may
not fit its target and a rounded binary-float result may not stay finite.

Use `catch` when this expression needs a local fallback:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count I32 = cast text catch then 0
]

The handler protects the conversion and evaluation of its operand:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    narrow |left U32, right U32| -> U8:
        value U8 = cast left + right catch then 0
        return value
    ;
]

This catches `U32` addition overflow and failure to convert the result to
`U8`. A conversion failure follows the ordinary implicit or typed error path
when no local handler catches it. A custom error slot never receives built-in
failure without explicit mapping.

Precision loss alone does not fail binary-float narrowing. The conversion
rounds directly to the destination and fails only if that rounded result is
non-finite. Binary-float-to-integer conversion truncates towards zero, then checks
the target range.

A catch cannot legalise an unsupported conversion or statically invalid
numeric work. See @./#fallible-casts (Fallible casts) for evidence and
compatibility rules.
